1. What are the softwares that helps to run java programs?
To run Java programs, you need a combination of development tools (to write and compile code) and a runtime environment (to execute it).
1. Java Development Kit (JDK)
2. Java Runtime Environment (JRE)
3. Integrated Development Environments (IDEs)
2.What is JDK and JRE?
(i) Java Development Kit (JDK): 
Contains the compiler (javac) to translate Java code into bytecode and the Java Runtime Environment (JRE) to run it.
  Examples:
Oracle JDK – Official JDK from Oracle.
OpenJDK – Free, open-source version of the JDK.
Amazon Corretto – OpenJDK build by Amazon.
  Without JDK, you cannot compile Java programs.
(ii)    Java Runtime Environment (JRE):
Runs  Java programs (already compiled), but cannot compile new code.
JRE Includes:
Java Virtual Machine (JVM) – Executes the compiled Java bytecode.
Core Java Class Libraries – Prebuilt classes (like java.util.*, java.io.*) that your programs use.
Supporting Files – Configuration files, security files, etc.




3. What is eclipse IDE?
Eclipse IDE is a free, open-source software application used by developers to write, compile, run, and debug programs, especially in Java (but it also supports many other languages).
Key Features
Code Editor – Highlights syntax, suggests code completions, and checks for errors.
Compiler Integration – Lets you build and run Java programs without opening a separate terminal.
Debugger – Helps you find and fix errors by running code step-by-step.
Plugins – Extend functionality for different languages (C, C++, Python, PHP, etc.).
Project Management – Organizes your files and libraries in one place.
Cross-platform – Works on Windows, macOS, and Linux.
In short:
If the JDK is the “engine” that makes Java run, Eclipse IDE is like the “car dashboard” that makes driving (coding) much easier.
4. How to run the java program in eclipse/netbeans IDE.
Step 1 – Install Java & Eclipse
Install the JDK (Java Development Kit).
Download Eclipse from https://www.eclipse.org/downloads/ and install it.
Step 2 – Open Eclipse & Create a Project
Launch Eclipse.
Go to File → New → Java Project.
Enter a Project Name (e.g., MyFirstProject).
Click Finish.
Step 3 – Create a Java Class
Right-click src folder → New → Class.
Enter a Class Name (e.g., HelloWorld).
Tick the box public static void main(String[] args) to create the main method automatically.
Click Finish.
Step 4 – Write Your Code
Step 5 – Run the Program
Click the green Run button  on the toolbar, or press Ctrl + F11.
Output appears in the Console window.
NETBEANS
Step 1 – Install Java & NetBeans
Install the JDK.
Download NetBeans from https://netbeans.apache.org/download/ and install it.
Step 2 – Create a New Project
Open NetBeans.
Go to File → New Project.
Under Java with Ant or Java with Maven, choose Java Application → Next.
Enter Project Name (e.g., MyFirstNetBeansProject).
Check Create Main Class and enter name (e.g., helloworld.HelloWorld).
Click Finish.
Step 3 – Write Your Code
Step 4 – Run the Program
Click the green Run button on the toolbar, or press Shift + F6.
Output appears in the Output window